cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  pointahead: the point-ahead angle for an optical crosslink, with every error
  term attributed to its own checkable line
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-24
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Computes the point-ahead angle for an optical inter-satellite link and returns
  an error budget in which each contribution is a separate, individually
  checkable line. The angle is computed two ways: a closed-form first-order
  solution, theta = 2 |v_rel_perp| / c about the instantaneous line of sight,
  and an iterated light-time reference that solves both light times against a
  declared trajectory model and applies the exact relativistic aberration with
  the local terminal's velocity. Over 300 seeded crosslinks spanning 494.5 to
  8268.0 km of range the closed form's worst disagreement with the reference on
  the point-ahead offset is 7.339728e-14 rad, which is 4.826409e-09 of the
  angle and is of the same order as the float64 direction resolution of the
  geometry, so the closed form wins and that is published as measured. The
  closed form does not degrade appreciably with range, because the trajectory
  curvature errors on the receive and transmit sides cancel to first order in
  the sum that forms the offset: the median error as a fraction of the angle is
  flat at 6.690292e-10 to 8.747972e-10 across six range bands. The magnitude is attributed
  to three sources, the light-time lead and the stellar and velocity aberration
  of the received and transmitted beams, and the attribution is shown to be
  frame-dependent while the total is not: across six inertial frames the total
  moves 3.858295e-14 rad while the light-time lead moves 4.510455e-05 rad, a
  ratio of 1.169e+09, and in the local terminal's rest frame both aberration
  lines are exactly zero. The uncertainty budget has six lines and every
  analytic line is verified against a numerical propagation of its own input
  over 1584 geometry-and-configuration combinations; 1548 pass and the 36 that
  fail are all the light-time iteration line at iteration cap zero, where the
  predicted error falls below the float64 direction resolution, and they are
  reported as failed rather than tuned away. Two of the analytic lines were
  wrong when first written and this check found both: a first-order-only
  ephemeris staleness model predicted 2.087363736690764e-22 rad against a
  measured
  2.3807507009717773e-08 rad, because for two terminals at equal radius under
  two-body
  gravity the relative acceleration is exactly along the line of sight and the
  jerk term carries the whole line; and the stale-position expansion needs a
  dt^3 / 3 coefficient rather than the dt^3 / 6 a plain Taylor series suggests.
  Hypothesis found six further defects, including a non-convergence of the
  universal-variable Kepler iteration over 88 revolutions of a 37.9 second
  orbit, and all ten are pinned as named regression tests with literal inputs.
  Aberration is implemented four times, once approximately and three times
  exactly by independent derivations, and the three exact routes agree to
  1.054369e-15 rad over 2000 random directions and boosts up to 0.95 c. astropy
  is the correct tool for aberration itself and is named and recommended in the
  README; it is not installed in the environment these numbers were measured in
  and no number here came from it, which is recorded as a deviation from the
  product specification. The total is always reported against the transmit beam,
  because the same 2.963074 microradian error costs 3.132821e-02 dB on a
  1550 nm, 10 mm-waist beam and 1.495885e+01 dB on a 1064 nm, 150 mm-waist one.
  Validation is level 2; 105 checks pass and 1 fails. Research grade. Not flight-qualified,
  not certified, not approved for operational aerospace use. No output of this
  package is suitable for an operational pointing decision.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - point-ahead-angle
  - optical-crosslink
  - laser-communication
  - aberration
  - light-time
  - pointing-acquisition-tracking
  - error-budget
references:
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Rindler
        given-names: "W."
    title: "Relativity: Special, General and Cosmological"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Oxford University Press"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      Section 3.6, the relativistic aberration relation
      cos(theta') = (cos theta - beta) / (1 - beta cos theta), implemented as
      pointahead.aberration.aberrate_angle_relation and cross-checked against
      two independent derivations to 1.054369e-15 rad. Structure only; no
      numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Moyer
        given-names: "T. D."
    title: >-
      Formulation for Observed and Computed Values of Deep Space Network Data
      Types for Navigation
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2003
    notes: >-
      JPL Deep-Space Communications and Navigation Series, Monograph 2,
      chapter 8, the light-time iteration used for observable computation and
      implemented as pointahead.lighttime.solve_light_time. Checked against the
      exact quadratic root for a straight-line trajectory to 0.000000e+00 worst
      relative difference over 400 cases.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Vallado
        given-names: "D. A."
    title: "Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications"
    edition: "4th"
    publisher:
      name: "Microcosm Press"
    year: 2013
    notes: >-
      Algorithm 8 (KEPLER) and section 2.2, universal-variable two-body
      propagation and the Stumpff functions, implemented as
      pointahead.kepler.propagate_two_body and checked against
      scipy.integrate.solve_ivp with DOP853 at rtol 1e-13 to 1.013e-13 worst
      relative position difference.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Siegman
        given-names: "A. E."
    title: "Lasers"
    publisher:
      name: "University Science Books"
    year: 1986
    notes: >-
      Section 17.1, the Gaussian far-field divergence half-angle
      lambda / (pi w0) to the 1/e^2 intensity radius, implemented as
      pointahead.divergence.gaussian_divergence_half_angle.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Andrews
        given-names: "L. C."
      - family-names: Phillips
        given-names: "R. L."
    title: "Laser Beam Propagation through Random Media"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "SPIE Press"
    year: 2005
    notes: >-
      Chapter 4, Gaussian beam propagation. The turbulence treatment that is the
      rest of the book does not apply to a space-to-space crosslink and is named
      in the README as a limitation for a space-to-ground one.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Klein
        given-names: "B. J."
      - family-names: Degnan
        given-names: "J. J."
    title: "Optical antenna gain. 1: Transmitting antennas"
    journal: "Applied Optics"
    volume: 13
    issue: 9
    year: 1974
    notes: >-
      Aperture truncation and central obscuration change the far-field pattern
      and the optimum truncation ratio. Neither is modelled here, and that is
      recorded in the README under Limitations.
  - type: book
    editors:
      - family-names: Hemmati
        given-names: "H."
    title: "Deep Space Optical Communications"
    publisher:
      name: "Wiley"
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      JPL Deep-Space Communications and Navigation Series, chapter 1, the
      point-ahead requirement as it appears in an optical link design. Structure
      only; no numerical value in this repository is taken from it.
  - type: software
    title: "sgp4"
    version: "2.27"
    notes: >-
      The reference SGP4/SDP4 implementation, wrapped by pointahead.tle rather
      than reimplemented, with the TEME frame label carried through to every
      result.
  - type: software
    title: "astropy"
    notes: >-
      The correct tool for aberration, light-time and frame transformations, and
      named as such in this package's README. NOT installed in the environment
      this package was measured in, and no number in this repository came from
      it; the aberration cross-check the product specification asks for was
      replaced by three internal exact implementations derived three different
      ways, which is recorded as a deviation in validation/VALIDATION.md.
